// Year 3 reading to greater depth: real classics (Kipling, Grahame, Carroll, Stevenson; Nesbit
// and Sewell as stretch), myths and legends retold for this app, new racing, space and superhero
// stories, fact files, figurative language, kinds of writing and three sets of wonderful words.
// Questions follow the KS2 reading domains: vocabulary, retrieval, summary, inference,
// prediction, the writer's choices, and comparison.

import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';

const PASSAGES = JSON.parse(readFileSync(new URL('./passages.json', import.meta.url), 'utf8'));
const P = (key) => ({ passage: PASSAGES[key].passage, passageSource: PASSAGES[key].passageSource });
const fact = (id, topic, prompt, answer, distractors, explain, extra = {}) => ({ id: `reading.y3.${id}`, topic: `reading.y3-${topic}`, kind: 'fact', prompt, answer, distractors, explain, ...extra });
const mcq = (id, topic, prompt, options, explain, extra = {}) => ({ id: `reading.y3.${id}`, topic: `reading.y3-${topic}`, kind: 'mcq', prompt, options: options.map(([text, correct, feedback]) => ({ text, correct: !!correct, feedback })), explain, ...extra });
const order = (id, topic, prompt, items, explain, extra = {}) => ({ id: `reading.y3.${id}`, topic: `reading.y3-${topic}`, kind: 'order', prompt, items, labels: null, explain, ...extra });
const choiceOnly = { formats: ['choice'] };
const own = 'A story written for this app';
const file = 'A fact file written for this app';
const myth = (who) => `A ${who} retold for this app`;

// --- myths and legends, retold ---------------------------------------------------------------
const THESEUS = { passage: 'Theseus and the Minotaur\n\nLong ago, on the island of Crete, there lived a cruel king called Minos. Deep beneath his palace was a maze called the Labyrinth, so twisting that nobody who went in could ever find the way out. In the middle of the maze lived the Minotaur, a monster that was half man and half bull.\n\nEvery year, King Minos made the city of Athens send fourteen young people to Crete, to be sent into the Labyrinth. None of them ever came back.\n\nOne year, Prince Theseus of Athens stepped forward. “I will go,” he said, “and I will stop the Minotaur for ever.”\n\nWhen he arrived in Crete, the king’s daughter, Ariadne, secretly came to him. She pressed a ball of golden thread into his hand. “Tie one end by the door,” she whispered, “and unwind it as you go.”\n\nTheseus crept into the darkness, unwinding the thread behind him. At the centre of the maze, the Minotaur charged, bellowing. Theseus was quick and strong, and after a long struggle the monster lay still.\n\nThen Theseus followed the golden thread, turn by turn, all the way back to the light.', passageSource: myth('Greek myth') };
const ICARUS = { passage: 'Icarus and the Wings\n\nDaedalus was the cleverest inventor in all of Greece. But King Minos of Crete had locked him in a tall tower with his son, Icarus, so that he could never leave the island.\n\n“Minos rules the land and the sea,” said Daedalus, “but he does not rule the sky.”\n\nFor many weeks he collected feathers dropped by the birds. He laid them in rows, the long ones and the short ones, and fixed them together with thread and wax. At last he had made two pairs of wings.\n\nHe fastened the wings to his son’s arms. “Listen carefully,” he said. “Do not fly too low, or the sea spray will make your feathers heavy. Do not fly too high, or the sun will melt the wax. Follow me.”\n\nThey leapt from the tower and soared out over the sea. Icarus whooped with joy. It was wonderful! He forgot his father’s warning and climbed higher and higher, up towards the blazing sun.\n\nThe wax grew soft. One by one, the feathers slipped away. Icarus flapped his bare arms, but it was no use. He tumbled down into the sea far below, and Daedalus flew on alone, weeping.', passageSource: myth('Greek myth') };
const ARTHUR = { passage: 'The Sword in the Stone\n\nWhen the old king of Britain died, he left no son to rule after him. The lords quarrelled and fought, and the land fell into trouble.\n\nThen, one winter morning, a strange thing appeared in a churchyard in London: a great stone, and stuck deep in the stone was a shining sword. Golden letters were written on it: “Whoever pulls this sword out of this stone is the true king of Britain.”\n\nOne by one, the strongest knights in the land took hold of the sword. They heaved and they strained until their faces turned red, but the sword did not move at all.\n\nOn New Year’s Day there was a great tournament. A boy called Arthur had come to London to help his older brother, Sir Kay. But Kay had left his sword at home! Arthur ran to fetch it, but the house was locked.\n\nHurrying back, Arthur passed the churchyard and saw the sword in the stone. “That will do for Kay,” he thought. He took hold of the handle and pulled. The sword slid out as easily as a spoon from a pot of honey.\n\nWhen the lords saw what Arthur had done, they knelt before him. The wizard Merlin smiled. He had known all along who the true king would be.', passageSource: myth('British legend') };
const GEORGE = { passage: 'St George and the Dragon\n\nOutside a town called Silene, there was a lake, and in the lake lived a dragon. Its breath was poisonous, and its roar shook the walls of the town. To keep the dragon away, the frightened people fed it two sheep every day.\n\nSoon there were no sheep left. The king was in despair, because now the dragon wanted a person instead. Lots were drawn, and the name that came out was the king’s own daughter, the princess.\n\nThe princess walked bravely down to the lake. Just then, a knight came riding by on a white horse. His name was George.\n\n“Why are you crying?” asked George. “Run away, good knight,” said the princess, “or the dragon will eat you too!”\n\nBut George did not run. When the dragon rose out of the water, he made the sign of the cross, lowered his lance and charged. The lance struck the dragon and it crashed to the ground.\n\nThe people of the town cheered and cheered. Today St George is the patron saint of England, and his day is celebrated on 23 April.', passageSource: myth('legend') };

// --- original stories -----------------------------------------------------------------------
const RAIN = { passage: 'The Rain Race\n\nThe sky over the Speedway was the colour of a bruise. Turbo sat on the grid, his engine ticking, while the crowd huddled under umbrellas.\n\n“It’s going to pour,” said Rosa over the radio. “We’ve put on the tyres with the deepest grooves. You’ll be slower at first, but trust them.”\n\nThe lights went out. Blaze shot ahead on his smooth dry-weather tyres, and Turbo slipped back to fifth. Two laps later, the clouds burst. Rain hammered on the track like a thousand drums.\n\nBlaze’s tyres could not grip. At the hairpin bend he slid wide, spinning gently round until he faced the wrong way. Turbo slowed, steered carefully round him and called on the radio, “Blaze has spun at the hairpin! Tell the marshals!”\n\nOne by one, the cars on dry tyres skated off the track. Turbo’s grooved tyres pushed the water away, and he crept up to second place, then first.\n\nAfter the race, a soggy Blaze rolled up beside him. “You could have just driven past me,” he said. “Why did you warn the marshals?”\n\nTurbo flashed his headlights. “Because winning only counts,” he said, “if everyone gets home safely.”', passageSource: own };
const METEOR = { passage: 'Captain Nova and the Meteor Storm\n\nThe alarm screeched across the bridge of the starship Comet. Bleep the robot spun round in circles, his lights flashing red.\n\n“Meteor storm!” he beeped. “Thousands of rocks, coming straight for us!”\n\nCaptain Nova studied the screen. The meteors were spread out like a swarm of angry bees, far too wide to fly around. Her ship’s shield was weak; a single big rock could crack it.\n\nThen she noticed something. Beside them floated a tiny grey moon, no bigger than a city.\n\n“Bleep,” she said calmly, “if we hide behind that moon, what will the meteors hit?”\n\nBleep’s lights flickered while he thought. “The moon!” he cried. “The moon will be our shield!”\n\nCaptain Nova steered the Comet into the moon’s shadow. Moments later, the storm arrived. Rocks pounded the far side of the little moon, sending up silent clouds of dust, but not one of them touched the ship.\n\nWhen the last meteor had whizzed past, Bleep did a happy spin. “How did you stay so calm?”\n\nCaptain Nova smiled. “Panicking uses up your brain,” she said. “Staying calm leaves room to think.”', passageSource: own };
const FLOOD = { passage: '{hero} and the Great Flood\n\nFor three days and nights the rain did not stop. On the fourth morning, the river burst its banks and brown water began to swirl through the streets of Millbrook.\n\n{hero} landed in the town square with a whoosh. “Everyone stay calm!” the hero called. “I’ll stop this flood!”\n\nBut there was too much water, even for a superhero. Each time {hero} pushed one wave back, two more rushed in somewhere else.\n\nThen an old man in a flat cap tapped the hero on the shoulder. “Begging your pardon,” he said, “but we’ve got thousands of sandbags at the builder’s yard. We just can’t carry them fast enough.”\n\n{hero} grinned. “Then let’s do it together!”\n\nThe people of Millbrook formed a long line, passing sandbags from hand to hand. {hero} flew the heaviest piles to the very front. Children carried small bags. Grandparents tied the knots. Slowly, a wall of sandbags grew along the river, higher and higher, until the water could not get over.\n\nThat evening, the whole town stood muddy and soaking wet, but smiling. “I’ve learned something today,” said {hero}. “Even a superhero is stronger with a team.”', passageSource: own };

// --- fact files ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
const ROMANS = { passage: 'Fact file: The Roman army\n\nThe Roman army was one of the best-trained armies in the ancient world. In AD 43, the Emperor Claudius sent it to invade Britain.\n\nThe army was split into legions. Each legion had about 5,000 soldiers, called legionaries. Legionaries were split into groups of about 80 men, each led by an officer called a centurion.\n\nA legionary carried a large curved shield, called a scutum, a short sword, a dagger and two throwing spears. He wore a metal helmet and armour made of metal strips.\n\nRoman soldiers could march about 20 miles in a day, carrying all their equipment on their backs. At the end of each day’s march, they built a camp with a ditch and a wall around it.\n\nIn battle, soldiers sometimes formed a “testudo”, which means tortoise. They held their shields above their heads and around their sides, so that arrows bounced off, like rain off a tortoise’s shell.\n\nGlossary\nlegion: a large group of Roman soldiers\ncenturion: an officer in charge of about 80 men', passageSource: file };
const PLANETS = { passage: 'Fact file: The planets\n\nOur Earth is one of eight planets that travel around the Sun. The time it takes a planet to go once around the Sun is called its year.\n\nThe four planets closest to the Sun, Mercury, Venus, Earth and Mars, are small and rocky. Mercury is the closest to the Sun and the smallest planet. Venus is the hottest, because its thick blanket of air traps the heat. Mars looks red because its dust contains rust.\n\nThe four outer planets, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus and Neptune, are giants made mostly of gas and ice. Jupiter is the biggest planet of all: more than a thousand Earths could fit inside it! Saturn has beautiful rings made of chunks of ice and rock. Neptune is the furthest from the Sun, so it is dark and freezing cold.\n\nEarth is the only planet where we know there is life. It is just the right distance from the Sun: not too hot and not too cold, so there is liquid water.', passageSource: file };
const BEES = { passage: 'Fact file: Honeybees\n\nHoneybees live together in a nest called a hive. A busy hive can hold more than 50,000 bees!\n\nEvery hive has three kinds of bee. There is one queen, who lays all the eggs. There are thousands of workers, which are all female. And there are some drones, which are male.\n\nThe workers do almost all the jobs. They clean the hive, look after the young bees, guard the entrance and fly out to collect nectar and pollen from flowers. They turn the sweet nectar into honey, which they store to eat in the winter.\n\nWhen a worker bee finds lots of flowers, she flies back to the hive and does a special dance, called the waggle dance. The dance tells the other bees which way to fly and how far to go.\n\nAs bees fly from flower to flower, they carry pollen with them. This helps the plants make seeds and fruit. Without bees, there would be far fewer apples, strawberries and tomatoes.', passageSource: file };

